novelty_contribution:
{
  "theoretical_contribution": "This study introduces the concept of enhanced prompting techniques as a mechanism to improve the internal representations of multilingual large language models (LLMs), positing that clearer internal representations can lead to safer outputs and increased user trust. It develops a theoretical framework linking interpretability with safety outcomes in multilingual contexts, addressing gaps in existing literature regarding the generalizability of safety measures.",
  "technical_contribution": "The research develops the multilingual safety benchmark (XSafety), a new dataset specifically designed to evaluate the safety of LLM outputs across multiple languages, facilitating a comprehensive assessment of safety measures in diverse cultural settings. Additionally, it employs output-level explanation and probing techniques to analyze internal representations, providing a methodological advancement in understanding model behavior.",
  "empirical_contribution": "Through a six-month evaluation involving users interacting with LLMs in four languages, the study empirically tests the effectiveness of enhanced prompting techniques against standard methods, generating quantitative data on unsafe response rates and qualitative insights into user trust and satisfaction. This contributes new empirical findings on the efficacy of safety interventions in multilingual environments.",
  "practical_deployment_contribution": "The findings will inform the design of automated customer support systems by providing concrete guidelines for implementing enhanced prompting techniques, ensuring safer interactions with LLMs. This practical contribution extends beyond theoretical implications by offering actionable strategies for improving user trust and satisfaction in real-world applications of multilingual LLMs."
}

